---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import UiLabel from "../../components/UiLabel.astro";
import HelpCard from "../../components/HelpCard.astro";

// Issue #1106, guide 6: moving your data — importing from Clue, Apple
// Health, or Health Connect, and exporting JSON, CSV, FHIR, or PDF. The
// export explanation reuses the in-app help card; screen names and button
// labels come from the ARB through <UiLabel>; every factual claim carries
// a `<!-- cite: ... -->` ledger entry checked by
// `site/scripts/check-claims.mjs`.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Moving your data"
  description="Bring your history into lunarlog from a Clue export or your phone's health app, and take all of it out again as JSON, CSV, FHIR, or a PDF — exports need no account."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Moving your data</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      Your history is yours: this guide covers bringing it in, keeping
      your phone's health app up to date, and getting it all back out.
      Everything here happens on your device, at your initiative —
      lunarlog never reads your other accounts, and it never touches your
      health store before you turn the connection on. Once a profile is
      bound and you have run the first import yourself, the import keeps
      itself current in the background: still on your device, and the
      import itself never writes.
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#7-data-export--retention--deletion-rights; PRIVACY.md#4-third-party-service-providers (the §4 background paragraph); lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart ("Bound. Two entry points."); ios/Runner/AppDelegate.swift (the HKObserverQuery trigger); android/app/src/main/kotlin/com/wjdavis5/lunarlog/HealthBackgroundImportWorker.kt (the periodic trigger) -->
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Bringing history in from Clue</h2>
      <p>
        lunarlog reads the export Clue emails you as a .zip, and turns it
        into a profile here. During setup, <UiLabel
          key="firstRunRestore"
        /> starts this; later, <UiLabel key="settingsSectionYourData" /> in
        <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" /> does — the picker explains both
        choices: <UiLabel key="importScreenPickBody" />
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart (restore entry); lib/ui/settings/settings_screen.dart (import tile); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (firstRunRestore, importScreenPickBody) -->
      </p>
      <ol class="doc__steps">
        <li>
          In Clue, request the export. Clue emails you a .zip together
          with a one-time password for it.
          <!-- cite: lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart (Clue export .zip + one-time password contract) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          In lunarlog, choose the downloaded .zip. When the app asks, enter
          the password from Clue's email into
          <UiLabel key="importClueExportPasswordLabel" /> and continue with
          <UiLabel key="importClueOpenExport" />.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (importClueExportPasswordLabel, importClueOpenExport, importCluePasswordPrompt); lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart -->
        </li>
        <li>
          The import creates its own profile —
          <UiLabel key="importClueNewProfileNote" /> — named from
          <UiLabel key="importClueProfileNameLabel" />. Confirm with
          <UiLabel key="importClueImport" />.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (importClueNewProfileNote/ProfileNameLabel/Import); lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart -->
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Anything already logged by hand in lunarlog is never overwritten
        by an import — your own entries stay yours; the import fills the
        gaps. A day Clue recorded that you have since re-logged here keeps
        your version.
        <!-- cite: lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart (additive merge rule); lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (the additive rule the Clue importer established) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>From Apple Health or Health Connect</h2>
      <p>
        On iPhone, lunarlog can import menstrual-flow history from Apple
        Health; on Android, from Health Connect — flow and spotting,
        decided by you, one profile per phone.
        <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (one shared pipeline, #217/#458); AGENTS.md (project overview) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        The switch lives in <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" /> under
        <UiLabel key="settingsHealthHeader" />:
        <UiLabel
          key="settingsHealthSyncTitle"
          values={{ source: "Apple Health" }}
        />
        — on Android the same tile names Health Connect — asks which
        profile's data this phone may sync. The import itself runs when
        you tap it: <UiLabel key="healthSyncImportTileSubtitle" />
        <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (settingsHealthSyncTitle, healthSyncImportTileSubtitle); lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart -->
      </p>
      <p>
        What the import reads, and what it never does:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It reads the flow and spotting history the health store makes
          available, not a recent window —
          <UiLabel key="healthSyncFullHistoryNote" />
          <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (whole-history read, issue #992); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncFullHistoryNote) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          It never overwrites a value you logged by hand, and it never
          writes its imports back into the health store — the app's own
          writes are excluded by source, so the two directions cannot
          loop.
          <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (merge rules, provenance, echo loop prevention) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          The first import is yours to start in Settings. Once a profile is
          bound and you have run the first import yourself, the same import
          also keeps itself current in the background — Apple Health tells
          lunarlog when new data lands, and Android checks Health Connect
          on a schedule — under the same rules: it never prompts, never
          shows a screen, never writes, and it stops if you unbind the
          profile or revoke the health permission.
          <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (importInBackground, #993: prompt-free, #153 guard + #959 permission probe); PRIVACY.md#4-third-party-service-providers (the §4 background paragraph) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          One profile is bound to the phone's health connection at a time;
          unbinding later stops both directions and deletes nothing.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/health/health_sync_binding.dart (#153 device binding); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncUnbindDialogImportBody) -->
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="health-app-writes">
      <h2>Keeping your phone's health app up to date</h2>
      <p>
        The same switch works in the other direction. For the one profile
        you choose there, what you log in lunarlog is also written to
        Apple Health on iPhone, or to Health Connect on Android, so the
        other health apps you use see the same history.
        <!-- cite: lib/config.dart (healthSyncWritePlatforms); lib/data/health/health_flow_write_service.dart; PRIVACY.md#4-third-party-service-providers -->
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It is off until you choose a profile, and it only ever writes
          that one profile. Every other profile on the phone stays out of
          the health app.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/health/health_sync_binding.dart (#153 device binding); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncWriteIntro, healthSyncWriteIntroHealthConnect) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Only days you log after it is on, and allowed to write, get an
          entry of their own. Nothing already in lunarlog is sent on its
          own. One thing reaches back on Android: once a day in a period
          is written, the first and last day you logged for that period
          are written with it, even if the period began earlier.
          <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_flow_write_service.dart (forward-only cursor, stamped when write access is granted); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncWriteForwardOnly, healthSyncWriteForwardOnlyHealthConnect, healthSyncPeriodRecordNoteHealthConnect) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Flow and spotting are written on both. On iPhone, so are the
          symptoms and moods you tag, cervical mucus, ovulation tests, and
          basal body temperature. On Android: each period's first and last
          day, cervical mucus, ovulation tests, and basal body temperature
          — but not symptoms or moods, because Health Connect has no
          categories for them; those stay in lunarlog.
          <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#4-third-party-service-providers; lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncWriteSymptoms, healthSyncWrittenTypesHealthConnect, settingsHealthSyncSymptomsAndroidLimitation) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          A super heavy day is written as Heavy: neither health app has a
          level above it.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncFlowCollapseNote, healthSyncFlowCollapseNoteHealthConnect) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Edit or delete a day in lunarlog and its copy in the health app
          follows.
          <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_flow_write_service.dart (re-write by record id, removed-record reconcile); lib/data/health/health_sync_tombstone_coordinator.dart -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Turning it off, or taking away lunarlog's access, stops new
          writes and leaves what was already written where it is. To
          remove that, delete it in the health app itself.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (healthSyncRevocationNote, healthSyncRevocationNoteHealthConnect) -->
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All of this happens on the phone, between lunarlog and the health
        app. It sends nothing to lunarlog's servers.
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#4-third-party-service-providers -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Taking your data out</h2>
      <HelpCard id="export-data" />
      <p>
        Every format lives under <UiLabel key="settingsSectionYourData" />
        → <UiLabel key="yourDataExportTitle" /> and its neighbours, all
        built on-device and handed to your share sheet:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><UiLabel key="yourDataExportTitle" /></strong> — a JSON
          file of every profile and day entry on the device, plus the
          account's server-side record when you are signed in. lunarlog
          can restore from its own backup the same way.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/export/account_export.dart (#17 U5, on-device); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (yourDataExportSubtitleSignedIn, yourDataImportSubtitle) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><UiLabel key="settingsCsvExportTitle" /></strong> —
          spreadsheet-friendly tables of the cycle data.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/export/csv_export.dart; lib/l10n/app_en.arb (settingsCsvExportTitle) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><UiLabel key="settingsClinicalExportFhirTitle" /></strong>
          — an IPS-shaped FHIR R4 document with coded, self-reported data,
          for a clinician's system.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/export/fhir_bundle.dart (#157); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (settingsClinicalExportSubtitleGeneric) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><UiLabel key="clinicalPdfExportTitle" /></strong> — a
          one-page PDF summary of the logged data, for an appointment.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/export/clinical_pdf.dart; lib/l10n/app_en.arb (clinicalPdfExportTitle) -->
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Exports work fully offline and need no account — the file is built
        from the copy on your device. On a shared or borrowed phone,
        remember the file carries everything that device can see; review
        it before passing it on.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (exportData, offlineMode cards); PRIVACY.md#7-data-export--retention--deletion-rights -->
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
